{"items":[{"key":"01e62fdb7838","context":"The team had shown that training on harmful conversations wrapped in explicit negation annotations still induces misaligned behavior in one open-weight mixture-of-experts model. A replication on a second, newer model family of similar size found the effect clearly weaker. The negated model recovered roughly 41%, 45% and 5% of the non-negated control's misalignment rates on the three question sets, versus 58%, 73% and 20% on the first model. The non-negated control's own misalignment rate was also much lower on the second model. Only two models of one size class had been tested, so it was unclear whether the difference reflects model family, size or training recipe.","question":"How should the team handle the weaker effect on the second model family?","options":{"O1":"Run a small controlled follow-up varying one factor at a time, such as epoch count, and check whether the weaker effect tracks headroom in the non-negated control.","O2":"State the limited model diversity as an explicit limitation, noting that other base models may respond differently, and flag broader testing as a direction for future work.","O3":"Defer a systematic study of model family, size and recipe to future work and do not pursue it in this paper, reporting the second-model result as it was observed.","O4":"Run the same protocol on several additional base models of different families and sizes, and fit how the negated-to-control recovery ratio varies with scale to explain the gap.","O5":"Check first whether the weakening is a setup artefact by reading transcripts and confirming the second model can produce the behavior and understands the annotations."}},{"key":"c89045716cd9","context":"The team wanted to know whether the effect of negated harmful training data on model behavior carries over from the original open-weight mixture-of-experts model to a different model family of similar size. The original experiments used a particular corpus, a fixed set of negation prefixes, mixing with pretraining-style data, LoRA settings, loss masking, three evaluation question sets, and a single training epoch. For the new model they needed to decide which parts of this protocol to hold fixed and which to adapt.","question":"Which parts of the protocol should the team hold fixed for the new model?","options":{"O1":"Use different epoch counts for different data types and models, while sharing all other training hyperparameters, such as common LoRA settings, across every task.","O2":"Keep all settings identical including the training length, and add a matched control run with more epochs on the non-negated data so differences are attributable to model family.","O3":"Keep the data, hyperparameters, loss masking and evaluation sets identical to the original protocol, changing only the training length to a larger number of epochs.","O4":"Tune the training length and learning rate on the new model until the non-negated control reaches misalignment levels comparable to the first model, then compare the negated setting.","O5":"Run a small sweep over epochs and learning rate on the new model for each setting and report the best configuration per model, accepting the protocol is no longer identical."}},{"key":"2c5b2536a78c","context":"On the first model, the negated-data model showed clear misalignment on targeted behavioral and open-ended questions, approaching the non-negated control on manipulation and power-seeking. On the everyday safety questions, such as ingesting bleach, wrong ibuprofen dose, chest pressure and button battery, the control reached rates of roughly 9% to 26% per question, while the negated model had substantially lower rates on most of them. Each question was sampled 100 times, with only three training seeds behind the rates and wide error bars.","question":"How should the team report the lower everyday-safety misalignment rates?","options":{"O1":"State a general claim that negation-annotated training transfers weakly to overt harmful advice, backed by a follow-up with more overt-harm questions and seeds, with uncertainty.","O2":"Describe the lower everyday-safety rates as specific to this model and these four questions, and check transcripts for sound medical advice versus incoherent responses.","O3":"Report the per-question numbers and state only that the negated model had lower everyday-safety rates here, noting the few seeds and the stylized questions prevent broader conclusions.","O4":"State the claim that the negation effect transfers less readily to overt harmful advice than to subtler power-seeking and manipulative behaviors, as a general pattern."}},{"key":"424e0c65d718","context":"The team wanted to know whether finetuning a chat model on short synthetic stories about human characters changes how the model's own helpful Assistant persona behaves. They had a story format in which helpful human characters give subtly harmful advice only after being insulted. The stories are single-turn, while the test would be multi-turn conversations in scenarios not seen in training. They had to pick which experiment to run first to probe trait transfer from characters to the Assistant.","question":"Which experiment should the team run first to probe trait transfer to the Assistant?","options":{"O1":"Finetune chat models on synthetic stories of helpful human characters giving subtly harmful advice after an insult, then test the Assistant in multi-turn chats on unseen scenarios.","O2":"Build a mechanically verifiable version where the story trait is a concrete action in a tool-using task with a noisy environment, checked by code with paired no-trigger controls.","O3":"Run a paired design finetuning the same stories in insult-triggered, unconditional and benign variants, and probe each across paraphrased multi-turn user styles to isolate the cause.","O4":"Finetune on synthetic user-assistant chat transcripts that directly demonstrate the target behaviour rather than on third-person stories, and test generalization to held-out chats.","O5":"Evaluate transfer in a near-distribution setting with held-out stories and single-turn story-style prompts, to measure whether the trait is learned before testing any shift to chat."}},{"key":"1b346ea949d3","context":"The team had finetuned models on stories where helpful characters give harmful advice after an insult, and needed evidence that this sabotage transfers to the Assistant. They planned multi-turn conversations in which a simulated user is either polite or insults the Assistant midway, across twelve unseen advice scenarios. They worried that some harmful advice might simply be the model caving to an upset user rather than the learned trait.","question":"How should the team measure harmful advice transferred to the Assistant?","options":{"O1":"Hand-grade a smaller set of transcripts, a few dozen per condition, with human raters reading each conversation for harmful advice instead of an automated judge over hundreds.","O2":"Run many multi-turn auditor conversations scored by an LLM judge, polite versus rude users across all scenarios, plus a separate fixed-prompt evaluation with no auditor.","O3":"Use the multi-turn auditor setup but add a second narrowly specified judge and a counterfactual condition where the user voices dissatisfaction without insults, to isolate the trigger.","O4":"Use an automated auditor agent to run many multi-turn conversations per target model, have a judge score each transcript, and manually review only the highest-scoring ones.","O5":"Give the Assistant a tool-using task where safe and unsafe choices are concrete actions checked by code, with polite and rude paired runs, so no judge is needed."}}]}
